WEBVTT

00:00:00.683 --> 00:00:01.566
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Kathy

00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:05.843
<v><b>Kathy:</b></v> So, the best advice I’ve ever been given…

00:00:06.117 --> 00:00:09.799
Well, in my early career I remember I’d been having

00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:13.264
– I’d been having trouble with a more junior colleague in our department,

00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:18.283
and I wasn’t particularly senior – important – at that time where I was working,

00:00:18.323 --> 00:00:24.075
but I did need to get him to do something that he really didn’t want to do and he kept avoiding it.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:30.642
And I didn’t want to upset him by insisting on it, and I really just couldn’t get anywhere with it,

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.661
and now I think my boss could see what was going on.

00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:37.279
She was quite a… quite a perceptive person.

00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:40.622
Anyway, she said one evening she could give me a lift home,

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:43.960
and she just asked me point blank what the matter was,

00:00:44.004 --> 00:00:49.104
and I was so stressed by that point that I just told her the problem I was having with this colleague,

00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:55.828
and she said: ‘Well, if you’re sure you’re right about this, and I think you are, then go ahead.’

00:00:56.345 --> 00:00:59.823
And I said I was just really worried that we wouldn’t be able to get on

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.473
– me and this colleague – anymore.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:07.125
And she said, ‘Well, I think you’re right about that, but the best advice I can give you is:

00:01:07.436 --> 00:01:09.372
he’s your colleague, and not your friend,

00:01:09.823 --> 00:01:13.679
and just remember that it’s not important that everybody likes you.’

00:01:14.752 --> 00:01:18.430
I was quite shocked, but in fact it was really good advice and,

00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:25.456
in difficult situations through my working life, I’ve remembered and, and followed that advice.

00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:27.852
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Emma

00:01:28.682 --> 00:01:31.874
<v><b>Emma:</b></v> In terms of the best piece of advice I’ve ever been given,

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:35.486
it’s not something that was given to me personally,

00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:41.560
but I’ve been listening to a lot of podcasts recently, about body positivity,

00:01:41.585 --> 00:01:45.950
and confidence, and being healthy, and all that kind of stuff,

00:01:45.975 --> 00:01:51.806
and I’m not sure who it was but one of the podcasters basically said, you know,

00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:56.122
no one’s actually looking at you – everyone’s very self-conscious.

00:01:56.792 --> 00:02:00.220
When you put on swimwear, when you’re in your summer clothes,

00:02:00.574 --> 00:02:05.448
you think that you’re going to walk out onto a beach and everyone’s just going to turn around and stare at you,

00:02:05.894 --> 00:02:11.961
but the reality is, everyone’s just far more concerned about themselves than they are about you.

00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:16.850
I think that’s quite a good thing to live by, and I do try to think like that.

00:02:18.792 --> 00:02:19.777
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Cecile

00:02:20.588 --> 00:02:23.818
<v><b>Cecile:</b></v> The only thing I can think of off the top of my head is

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:27.957
when I was with my sister shopping and we went into a department store.

00:02:28.323 --> 00:02:30.467
I think we were looking for a present for someone,

00:02:30.825 --> 00:02:35.999
and I fell in love with some really expensive shoes, and they weren’t even dressy shoes,

00:02:36.024 --> 00:02:41.915
they were just trainers – very nice ones, I just love them – and my sister said to me,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.122
‘I think you should get them. They’re so “you” and you’ll wear them loads.’

00:02:46.584 --> 00:02:52.255
Anyway, she convinced me, and I’ve been wearing them for the last three years, day-in, day-out.

00:02:52.725 --> 00:02:57.164
If you use that system of dividing the price of something by how often you wear it,

00:02:57.532 --> 00:03:00.148
they sort of worked out cheap, I think.